@title PCSA00015 boot - splash, autosave notice, title screen, loading
@game  PCSA00015
@shot-every 10000

# ============================================================================
# WHAT THIS RECIPE DOES
# Boots the title and records how far it gets, with no input at all. It is the
# baseline every deeper PCSA00015 recipe builds on, and it deliberately sends
# NOTHING - see "input" below for why that is not an oversight.
#
# WHAT IS MEASURED (2026-07-28, all of it from runs, none of it assumed)
# - The title reaches `main`, renders, and hits ZERO unimplemented NIDs over
#   100,000 display flips.
# - Screen order, all rendering correctly:
#     the SCE publisher splash
#     -> "THIS GAME SAVES DATA AUTOMATICALLY AT CERTAIN POINTS" + autosave icon
#     -> the WIPEOUT logo with the copyright line
#     -> a LOADING screen with a real percentage and progress bar (94% at
#        f60000)
# - It plays audio from the title screen on (SceNgs voices are set up and the
#   AT9 path is fed).
#
# INPUT: THE TITLE IS TOUCH-DRIVEN, NOT BUTTON-DRIVEN.
# Measured with `explore` against a no-input control at the autosave notice:
# `cross`, `circle` and `start` produced determinism signatures BIT-IDENTICAL to
# doing nothing; a touch did not. Do not waste a session mapping buttons here.
#
# AND BEFORE TRYING TO DISMISS A SCREEN WITH INPUT AT ALL: this title sat on the
# autosave notice for 42 minutes of game time and it was NOT waiting for input.
# `sceAppUtilSaveDataDataSave` was writing its autosave where the title's own
# `sceIoGetstat` could not read it back, so it saved, looked, failed and saved
# again forever. A screen that does not advance is a SAVE or a LOAD before it is
# an input problem - `RUST_LOG=vitaslop::err=debug` then `vitaslop::io=trace`
# names it in two runs.
#
# CAVEAT that decides how to read a stall on a touch-driven screen:
# `vita::gesture` reports POSITION-ONLY gesture events and cannot tell a tap
# from a drag from a pinch (SceSystemGesture has no published struct layout, so
# the type values are known but their meanings are not). If a menu ignores a
# scripted tap, suspect that before suspecting the tap coordinates.
# ============================================================================

# >>> THIS RECIPE IS THE BOOT LADDER ONLY. To reach the front end, use
# >>> front-end.recipe, and read its header first - it needs
# >>> `VITASLOP_FRAME_TOPUP=0`, and so does anything that wants this title to
# >>> finish loading at all.
#
# WITH THE DEFAULT CLOCK (top-up on) THIS TITLE NEVER FINISHES LOADING.
# Measured: a 300,000-frame run ends still on the loading screen. A displayed
# frame carries about a TENTH of the guest work a console frame carries, because
# a display flip tops the game clock up to a full frame however little guest
# code actually ran (3120 fps, barely one whole scheduler quantum per frame).
# With `VITASLOP_FRAME_TOPUP=0` the same boot reaches "PRESS ANY BUTTON TO
# START" by f30000. See `VitaState::advance_time_frame`.
#
# AND THE LOADING IS NOT I/O-BOUND - the 28a note that said so is REFUTED. With
# the storage model switched OFF entirely (`VITASLOP_IO_BANDWIDTH_KIBPS=0`),
# f30000 reaches the same 94% that 50 MiB/s reaches; 10 MiB/s reaches 87%. An
# io trace over 90,000 frames reads 291 MiB in total, which is 29 seconds of
# modelled storage time against 25 minutes of game time.

0: @section boot
# No input. The whole point of this recipe is the unattended boot ladder.

# @todo No @assert yet: there is no progress value pinned down to assert on.
# The loading percentage on screen is the obvious candidate. NOTE a scan for it
# was tried and produced a FALSE POSITIVE that survived two observations
# (0x9697aa28 read 56 then 94 at the right frames, then read 99 while the screen
# read 75) - so whatever is found this way must be checked against the SCREEN at
# the same instant before it is trusted.
100000: @section loaded
